#open-question(title: [Which Treatment Probes Would Test the Recovery-Ratio Mechanism?])[
If the recovery ratio measures a real process — perfusion, neuromuscular transmission, or substrate refilling — then a drug that acts on that process should move the ratio selectively, without necessarily changing maximal force. That logic generates testable probe designs, none of which has been run with HGS as the endpoint.

- *Pyridostigmine* (acetylcholinesterase inhibition, prolongs acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction) is already studied in ME/CFS @Schlomer2025PyridostigmineMECFS. A recovery-ratio-selective response would implicate neuromuscular transmission rather than muscle energetics; a strength-only response would not.
- *Immunoadsorption* removes circulating β-adrenergic receptor autoantibodies and has been studied in ME/CFS @Scheibenbogen2025immunoadsorption @Stein2024immunoadsorption. If recovery ratio improves only in autoantibody-positive patients, the ratio becomes a candidate response marker for an antibody-defined subgroup.
- *Creatine monohydrate* supports phosphocreatine resynthesis. It has been tested in ME/CFS with brain magnetic resonance spectroscopy as the mechanistic endpoint @Godlewska2024creatineMRS, and in post-COVID fatigue with grip strength among the outcomes @DosSantos2026creatineCovid. A selective effect on the *fatigue* ratio, rather than on resting force, would point to substrate limitation within the session.
- *Citrulline malate* is a nitric-oxide precursor marketed for exercise recovery @Bendahan2002citrulline. Any effect in ME/CFS would need to be distinguished from a general training effect before it could be read as perfusion-specific.
- *Iron repletion* in patients with low ferritin is a plausible oxygen-delivery intervention, but ferritin is an acute-phase reactant and a low value in ME/CFS does not establish iron deficiency at the muscle — the ferritin association reported in the post-COVID cohort was confined to men and was modest (r = 0.42) @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS.

Each design requires the same safeguards: the endpoint must be pre-registered, the patient must be tested at the same time of day, and the analysis must separate force from ratio. No trial has done this, so the discriminating value of every probe above is currently zero — they are candidate designs, not evidence.

*Prediction:* at least one probe (most plausibly pyridostigmine) will move the recovery ratio selectively without changing maximal force, and the effect will be confined to the mechanistically predicted subgroup. *Falsified if:* no probe separates the ratio from force, or every observed effect is strength-only.

*Certainty: 0.20* for the probe logic (drug mechanisms are established; the HGS-endpoint inference is untested in ME/CFS).

*Consequence:* These designs would tell clinicians whether a drug response means "the muscle recovers better" or "the patient can push harder today" — two very different reasons to continue a treatment.
] <oq:handgrip-treatment-probes>
